F14.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F15.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F16.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F17.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F2.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F3.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F4.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F5.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F6.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F7.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F8.raw\nMS192164_HCD_F9.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R1.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R10.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R11.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R12.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R13.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R14.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R15.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R16.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R17.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R18.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R19.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R2.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R20.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R3.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R4.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R5.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R6.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R7.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R8.raw\nMS192164_HCD_R9.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nThe CX-MS-based quantifitative proteomic analysis of interactome of plant nucleus\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nTo profile the interactome of cell nucleus,a formaldehyde-CBDPS double chemical-crosslinking method was combined with mass spectrometry to perform quantitative interactomics on the in vivo fixed nuclei.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe soybean plants were divided into two groups,one of which was treated with 5 ppm ethylene and the other was treated with air. The plants grew for 7 days in darkness. The etiolated hypocotyls of both the air and ethylnene-treated seedlings were harvested, immediately sliced into 1.5-mm thick discs and subsequently. Next, the discs were infiltrated with 0.2% formaldehyde. The protein extaction and digestion was performed, followed by dimethyl labelling and fraction by SCX-HPLC. The CX-peptides were enricheed using strepavidin resions. The samples were then subjected for LC-MS/MS analysis. For LC-MS/MS analysis, peptides were separated by a 120 min gradient elution at a flow rate of 0.3µL/min with a Thermo-Dionex Ultimate 3000 HPLC system., which directly interfaced with a Thermo Orbitrap Fusion Lumos mass spectrometer. The analytical column was the Acclaim PepMapTM RSLC C18 Capillary column (75 µm ID, 150 mm length; Thermo Scientific Inc., Waltham, MA, USA). Mobile phase A consisted of 0.1% FA, and mobile phase B consisted of 100% ACN and 0.1% FA.  For the CID MS/MS experiment, we selected the ten most abundant precursors and subjected them to sequential CID MS/MS and ETD-MS/MS acquisitions. All spectral data were acquired in the Orbitrap mass analyzer. For the MS scans, the scan range was set to 300 - 2000 m/z at a resolution of 120K, the automatic gain control target was set to 500,000 and the charge state was set as +4 to +8. For the MS/MS scans, the resolution was set to 30,000, the automatic gain control target was set to 300,000, the precursor isolation width was 1.6 Da, and the maximum injection time was set to 54ms. The CID normalized collision energy was 30%; the charge-dependent Electron Transfer Dissociation (ETD) reaction time was enabled; the orbitrap resolution was 30K; the ETD automatic gain control target was set to 300,000; the scan range mode was auto normal; the precursor isolation width was 1.6 Da; and the maximum injection time was set to 54 ms. For the HCD MS/MS experiment, the settings were the same as the CID MS/MS except the MS/MS activation type was set as HCD and the collision energy was 26%.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nGlycine max\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
