2026-06-30 15:27:22,927 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-de8429fc-41f1-48e3-ae9f-61d925909280', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: disease\nsdrf_column: characteristics[disease]\nontology: EFO:0000408, MONDO, DOID, PATO\ndescription: Disease state of the sample.\ninstructions: For healthy/normal samples use "normal" (PATO:0000461). For diseased\n  samples use MONDO or DOID terms. Return null only if completely unstated.\n  Most XL-MS samples are "normal" unless explicitly studying a disease model.\n\nterm: phenotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phenotype]\nontology: EFO:0000651, PATO, EFO\ndescription: Observable characteristics or traits (drug sensitivity, molecular markers,\n  expression phenotypes, environmental responses).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. This captures how the sample behaves/appears\n  (distinct from disease diagnosis).\n\nterm: treatment\nsdrf_column: characteristics[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Treatment or perturbation applied to the sample (drug, stimulus,\n  environmental stress, growth condition change).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable. Examples: "doxycycline\n  treatment", "heat shock", "DMSO vehicle control".\n\nterm: compound\nsdrf_column: characteristics[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:24431, CHEBI, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Chemical compound or drug applied to the sample.\ninstructions: Use ChEBI if possible. Return null if no compound treatment. Do NOT\n  include the crosslinking reagent here — that goes in comment[cross-linker].\n\nterm: dose\nsdrf_column: characteristics[dose]\nontology: NCIT:C25488\ndescription: Dose or concentration of compound treatment.\npattern: number + unit (e.g. 10 mg/kg, 50 uM, 100 nM, 10 mg/mL)\ninstructions: Return null if no compound treatment or dose not stated.\n\nterm: genetic modification\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genetic modification]\nontology: EFO:0000510, EFO\ndescription: Method of genetic modification (knockout, knockdown, overexpression,\n  transduction). Describes HOW the modification was done.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: perturbation\nsdrf_column: characteristics[perturbation]\nontology: PRIDE:0000700\ndescription: Type of perturbation applied (environmental/experimental perturbation).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: depletion\nsdrf_column: characteristics[depletion]\nontology: PRIDE:0000020\ndescription: Whether abundant protein depletion was performed.\nallowed_values: no depletion, depletion, not applicable\ninstructions: Return "no depletion" if not mentioned. Return "depletion" if\n  high-abundance protein removal is described (e.g. albumin depletion).\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nThe CX-MS-based quantifitative proteomic analysis of interactome of plant nucleus\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nTo profile the interactome of cell nucleus,a formaldehyde-CBDPS double chemical-crosslinking method was combined with mass spectrometry to perform quantitative interactomics on the in vivo fixed nuclei.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe soybean plants were divided into two groups,one of which was treated with 5 ppm ethylene and the other was treated with air. The plants grew for 7 days in darkness. The etiolated hypocotyls of both the air and ethylnene-treated seedlings were harvested, immediately sliced into 1.5-mm thick discs and subsequently. Next, the discs were infiltrated with 0.2% formaldehyde. The protein extaction and digestion was performed, followed by dimethyl labelling and fraction by SCX-HPLC. The CX-peptides were enricheed using strepavidin resions. The samples were then subjected for LC-MS/MS analysis. For LC-MS/MS analysis, peptides were separated by a 120 min gradient elution at a flow rate of 0.3µL/min with a Thermo-Dionex Ultimate 3000 HPLC system., which directly interfaced with a Thermo Orbitrap Fusion Lumos mass spectrometer. The analytical column was the Acclaim PepMapTM RSLC C18 Capillary column (75 µm ID, 150 mm length; Thermo Scientific Inc., Waltham, MA, USA). Mobile phase A consisted of 0.1% FA, and mobile phase B consisted of 100% ACN and 0.1% FA.  For the CID MS/MS experiment, we selected the ten most abundant precursors and subjected them to sequential CID MS/MS and ETD-MS/MS acquisitions. All spectral data were acquired in the Orbitrap mass analyzer. For the MS scans, the scan range was set to 300 - 2000 m/z at a resolution of 120K, the automatic gain control target was set to 500,000 and the charge state was set as +4 to +8. For the MS/MS scans, the resolution was set to 30,000, the automatic gain control target was set to 300,000, the precursor isolation width was 1.6 Da, and the maximum injection time was set to 54ms. The CID normalized collision energy was 30%; the charge-dependent Electron Transfer Dissociation (ETD) reaction time was enabled; the orbitrap resolution was 30K; the ETD automatic gain control target was set to 300,000; the scan range mode was auto normal; the precursor isolation width was 1.6 Da; and the maximum injection time was set to 54 ms. For the HCD MS/MS experiment, the settings were the same as the CID MS/MS except the MS/MS activation type was set as HCD and the collision energy was 26%.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
