2026-06-30 15:27:39,116 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-da4cbcca-0591-401e-b913-d27b9c7d3454', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: sex\nsdrf_column: characteristics[sex]\nontology: PATO:0000047\ndescription: Biological sex of the donor.\nallowed_values: male, female, intersex, hermaphrodite, anonymized, not available, not applicable\ninstructions: Use one of the fixed values above. Return "not available" if not stated.\n  Return "not applicable" if the sample is from a cell line or organism without defined sex.\n\nterm: age\nsdrf_column: characteristics[age]\nontology: EFO:0000246\ndescription: Age of the donor at sample collection.\npattern: {Number}{Unit} where Unit is Y (years), M (months), W (weeks), D (days).\n  Also allowed: "anonymized" if age is stated but anonymized.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: ancestry category\nsdrf_column: characteristics[ancestry category]\nontology: HANCESTRO\ndescription: Ancestry or ethnic background of the donor.\ninstructions: Use HANCESTRO terms if possible. Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: biological replicate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[biological replicate]\nontology: MS:1001809\ndescription: Identifier for the biological replicate (integer or "pooled").\ninstructions: Return a description of the replication strategy from the paper\n  (e.g. "3 biological replicates", "2 independent experiments"). The actual\n  integer values are assigned per-file automatically; describe what you find in the text.\n\nterm: genotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genotype]\nontology: EFO:0000513\ndescription: Known genetic variant, mutation, or genotype of the subject.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Example: "wild type", "Δatg5", "ΔYme1".\n\nterm: pooled sample\nsdrf_column: characteristics[pooled sample]\nontology: NCIT:C165587\ndescription: Whether the sample is a pooled sample.\nallowed_values: not pooled, pooled, or pattern SN=sample1;SN=sample2\ninstructions: Return "not pooled" unless explicitly stated otherwise.\n\nterm: phosphate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phosphate]\nontology: PRIDE:0000805\ndescription: Phosphate concentration (only relevant for environmental/water samples).\npattern: number + mg/L or umol/L\ninstructions: Return null unless this is a water/environmental sample with explicit\n  phosphate concentration stated.\n\nterm: temperature\nsdrf_column: characteristics[temperature]\nontology: PRIDE:0000703\ndescription: Temperature at the sampling location (environmental samples only).\npattern: number + °C\ninstructions: Return null unless this is an environmental/metaproteomics sample\n  with explicit temperature stated. Do NOT confuse with crosslinking temperature.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nThe CX-MS-based quantifitative proteomic analysis of interactome of plant nucleus\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nTo profile the interactome of cell nucleus,a formaldehyde-CBDPS double chemical-crosslinking method was combined with mass spectrometry to perform quantitative interactomics on the in vivo fixed nuclei.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe soybean plants were divided into two groups,one of which was treated with 5 ppm ethylene and the other was treated with air. The plants grew for 7 days in darkness. The etiolated hypocotyls of both the air and ethylnene-treated seedlings were harvested, immediately sliced into 1.5-mm thick discs and subsequently. Next, the discs were infiltrated with 0.2% formaldehyde. The protein extaction and digestion was performed, followed by dimethyl labelling and fraction by SCX-HPLC. The CX-peptides were enricheed using strepavidin resions. The samples were then subjected for LC-MS/MS analysis. For LC-MS/MS analysis, peptides were separated by a 120 min gradient elution at a flow rate of 0.3µL/min with a Thermo-Dionex Ultimate 3000 HPLC system., which directly interfaced with a Thermo Orbitrap Fusion Lumos mass spectrometer. The analytical column was the Acclaim PepMapTM RSLC C18 Capillary column (75 µm ID, 150 mm length; Thermo Scientific Inc., Waltham, MA, USA). Mobile phase A consisted of 0.1% FA, and mobile phase B consisted of 100% ACN and 0.1% FA.  For the CID MS/MS experiment, we selected the ten most abundant precursors and subjected them to sequential CID MS/MS and ETD-MS/MS acquisitions. All spectral data were acquired in the Orbitrap mass analyzer. For the MS scans, the scan range was set to 300 - 2000 m/z at a resolution of 120K, the automatic gain control target was set to 500,000 and the charge state was set as +4 to +8. For the MS/MS scans, the resolution was set to 30,000, the automatic gain control target was set to 300,000, the precursor isolation width was 1.6 Da, and the maximum injection time was set to 54ms. The CID normalized collision energy was 30%; the charge-dependent Electron Transfer Dissociation (ETD) reaction time was enabled; the orbitrap resolution was 30K; the ETD automatic gain control target was set to 300,000; the scan range mode was auto normal; the precursor isolation width was 1.6 Da; and the maximum injection time was set to 54 ms. For the HCD MS/MS experiment, the settings were the same as the CID MS/MS except the MS/MS activation type was set as HCD and the collision energy was 26%.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
